    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d564">

                <pb facs="1009" n="995" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg995"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.48 Refugees/5272</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Apostolic Delegate</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Cicognani</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Acting Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 581/42</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-26">February 26,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Stettinius</hi>: Referring to your
                    letter of February 21, 1944, and my reply of February 23, 1944,<anchor n="36"
                        xml:id="d564-f1"/> concerning the welfare of the Jews remaining in Slovakia,
                    I beg to inform you that I am in receipt of a reply from the Cardinal Secretary
                    of State on this question.</p>
                <p>His Eminence, the Cardinal, states that he has interested the Papal
                    Representative in Bratislava in the condition of these Jews. The Representative
                    sent a communication to the Secretariate of State to the effect that, although
                    the present condition of the Jews in Slovakia <pb facs="1010" n="996"
                        xml:id="pg996"/> is indeed sad and still uncertain, the census that was
                    taken had only one purpose, the investigation of certain specific suspicions.
                    Furthermore, according to a promise from the President of the Republic, Dr.
                    Josepf Tiso, the Jews will not be persecuted, that is, will not be condemned to
                    severe punishments, but will only be interned and will be given opportunity and
                    facilities to withdraw to some other country.</p>
                <p>In bringing you this communication from the Apostolic Nunciature in Bratislava I
                    wish to assure you that the Holy See will continue its interest in the welfare
                    of these unfortunate people.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d564-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>With highest personal regard and sentiments of esteem, I beg to remain</p>
                <p>Yours very sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>A. G. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cicognani</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Archbishop of Laodicea</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d564-f1" n="36" xml:id="d564-f1-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f2" n="37" xml:id="d564-f2-content">
                <p>The Apostolic Delegate, in his letter of May 24, not printed, advised that
                    Slovakian authorities had given assurances to the Apostolic Nunciature in
                    Slovakia that, despite the registration of Jews, officials had no intention of
                    taking any other anti-Semitic measures; the Nunciature added, however, that the
                    possibility of new reprisals could not be excluded.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
